tel 1.0

base sibylline
import soundness

info   typed conversations with language models: sessions, streaming, tools as Scala methods, structured answers and batches
detail
    _Sibylline_ talks to large language models through one vocabulary, whatever the provider.
    A provider and model are a plain value (`Anthropic`, `OpenAI`, `Gemini`, or an
    OpenAI-compatible server such as vLLM, Ollama or Groq), tuned by methods which each return
    a new value; a conversation is an `Llm.Session`, opened on that value with `session` and
    reached inside the block as `llm`, which accumulates its history and token usage; a turn
    returns an `Llm.Reply`, pure data which says why the model stopped; streamed output is an
    `Llm.Response` whose text is pulled off the live connection; a tool is an ordinary Scala
    method annotated `@ability`, gathered into a `Toolkit` whose JSON schemas are derived from
    the parameter types; and `solicit[T]` asks for an answer of a type and decodes the document
    the model supplies against a schema derived from `T`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
    import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
    import internetAccess.online
    import logging.silentLogging
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import codepages.utf8Codepage

    val claude = Anthropic(t"claude-sonnet-4-5", t"sk-ant-…").prompted(t"Be terse.").limit(1024)
    ```

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    val reply: Llm.Reply = claude.session:
      llm.ask(t"What is the tallest mountain in Estonia?")
      llm.ask(t"And how tall is it?")

    reply.text
    reply.stop
    reply.usage
    ```

    Each provider's API is a JSON dialect: a request with the model, the messages so far and a
    few tuning knobs; a reply with content blocks, a stop reason and token counts; a streaming
    variant of server-sent events; and a tool-calling protocol. The dialects differ in every
    detail and agree in every concept, so the concepts are modelled once — `Llm.Message`,
    `Llm.Content`, `Llm.Reply`, `Llm.Usage`, `Llm.Tool`, `Llm.Stop`, `Llm.Event` — and
    translated onto each wire by a private `Llm.Dialect` the provider's `Sessional` constructs.
    A block the wire does not model is kept verbatim as `Llm.Content.Opaque`, so a transcript
    never lies.

    A session is a capability confined to its block by capture checking: the pure values it
    produces (a `Reply`, a snapshot of `history`, the folded `usage`) leave freely, while the
    session itself and any `Response` in flight cannot be returned or stashed, so a connection
    cannot be held open by accident, and a new turn cannot be started while a stream is
    undrained. A `Toolkit` given in scope turns `ask` into the whole tool loop: the model's
    calls are run, their results reported, and the conversation continued until the model
    answers in text or the `iterating` bound trips.

    Every failure is an `Llm.Error` (error 990) whose `Reason` is one of a fixed set, with the
    HTTP status and any `retry-after` delay; a rate limit or an overload is retried three times
    before it is raised. A call needs an `Online` capability (`internetAccess.online`), an
    `Http.Backend` (`httpBackends.javaNetHttp`), an `Http.Event is Loggable`
    (`logging.silentLogging`) and a `Tactic[Llm.Error]`, all of which the provider's
    `sessional` given takes from scope when `session` is called; a `Toolkit` is the one given
    a session reads at each `ask`, and `Toolkit.empty` applies when none is given.

    The library is one source set, `sibylline.core`, for the JVM: every call is a synchronous
    round trip on the caller's thread, and a retry or a batch's `await` sleeps the thread.
    `Anthropic`, `Gemini`, `OpenAI`, `Llm`, `Toolkit`, `ability`, `llm` and `solicit` are
    exported to the `soundness` umbrella. The `@about` annotation which describes a tool is
    synesthesia's, so one description serves a method exposed both as an MCP tool and as a
    model ability.

refer sibylline.Llm
refer sibylline.Anthropic
refer sibylline.OpenAI
refer sibylline.Gemini
refer sibylline.Toolkit
refer sibylline.llm
refer sibylline.solicit

entry .llm   the session a `provider.session:` block lends, as a contextual accessor
    `llm` is the `Llm.Session` the enclosing `session` block was given: `llm.ask(…)`,
    `llm.stream(…)`, `llm.solicit[T](…)`, `llm.history` and `llm.usage`. It is a named
    contextual parameter, not a `summon`, so it refers to the very session the block owns, and
    its result captures that session, which keeps it confined to the block.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    claude.session:
      llm.ask(t"Hello.")
      llm.usage
    ```

entry .solicit   a structured answer of a named type, decoded from the document the model supplies
    An extension on `Llm.Session`. `llm.solicit[T](prompt)` derives the JSON schema of `T`
    (`T is Schematic over JsonSchema`, derived for a case class), sends `prompt` as the user
    turn, and decodes the reply to `T` (`T is Decodable in Json`). How the schema reaches the
    model is the dialect's business: on a dialect whose wire constrains a reply natively
    (`Anthropic`, through the Messages API's output format) it is the reply's `format`; on the
    others the model is forced to call one synthetic tool named `answer` whose arguments have
    that schema, and the arguments are decoded. The caller names a type and receives a value,
    on every provider, including local servers with no structured-output mode.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Appraisal(ticker: Text, rating: Text, confidence: Double)
    ```

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    claude.session:
      llm.solicit[Appraisal](t"Summarize your recommendation for AAPL.")
    ```

    A reply which ignores the forced tool, which is not JSON, or whose document does not decode
    to `T` raises `Llm.Error` with the reason `Malformed`, through the session's own tactic, so
    a malformed answer is an error to handle rather than a value which looks right. The turn is
    committed to the history like any other.

entry .ability   marks a method as a tool the model may call, for `Toolkit(…)` to derive
    A `StaticAnnotation`, written `@ability` on a method of the object a `Toolkit` is built
    from. The method's first parameter list becomes the tool's JSON schema (each parameter
    type needs a `Schematic over JsonSchema` and a `Decodable in Json`), its return type is
    encoded as the result (`Encodable in Json`), and a second, contextual parameter list is
    summoned where `Toolkit(…)` is written. Its description is synesthesia's `@about(text)`,
    shared with the MCP integration; a method without one has an empty description. The name
    is `ability`, not `tool`, which synesthesia already exports.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    object Broker:
      @ability
      @about(t"Look up the current price of a stock ticker")
      def price(ticker: Text): Double = if ticker == t"AAPL" then 211.5 else 100.0

    Toolkit(Broker).specs.map(_.name)
    ```

entry .Toolkit   the tools a conversation offers its model: their specifications and a dispatcher
  detail
      A `Toolkit` is what a session reads, as a given, at every `ask`: `#specs` are the
      `Llm.Tool`s offered to the model, and `#invoke` runs the one the model named with the
      JSON arguments it supplied. With a toolkit given in scope, `ask` runs the whole loop —
      the model's calls are invoked, each result reported back as an `Llm.Content.ToolResult`,
      and the exchange re-sent — until the model stops for a reason other than `ToolCall` or the
      `iterations` bound trips with `Llm.Error(ToolLoopExceeded)`. With no given in scope
      `.empty` applies, and a `ToolCall` reply is returned to the caller to handle.

      `Toolkit(target)` derives one from the `@ability`-annotated methods of an object:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      object Broker:
        @ability
        @about(t"Look up the current price of a stock ticker")
        def price(ticker: Text): Double = if ticker == t"AAPL" then 211.5 else 100.0

      val broker: Toolkit = Toolkit(Broker)
      broker.specs.map(_.name)                                // List(t"price")
      broker.invoke(t"price", j"""{"ticker": "AAPL"}""")   // j"211.5"
      ```

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      claude.session:
        given Toolkit = broker
        llm.ask(t"Compare AAPL and MSFT.")   // calls price twice, then answers in text
      ```

      A failure inside `invoke` — an unknown tool, a missing or malformed argument, or an
      error the method itself raised — reaches the model as a tool result marked as an error,
      which it can correct itself from, rather than ending the conversation. A textual result
      is sent as prose, not as a JSON-quoted string. Every turn the loop makes is committed to
      the session's history, so the record shows the calls the model made and what it was told.

      A toolkit built where a capability is in scope captures it (a contextual parameter is
      summoned at the construction site), and capture checking confines the toolkit
      accordingly; a toolkit of pure methods is a plain value which may be shared.
  refer sibylline.ability
  refer sibylline.Llm.Tool
  entry .empty   the toolkit a session has unless another is given: no tools
      The default given, in `Toolkit`'s companion: `specs` is empty and `invoke` raises
      `Llm.Error(Invalid)`, _no tool named `name` is defined_, for a call which should never
      arrive. A `given Toolkit = Toolkit(…)` in the session block outranks it.
  entry .apply   derives a toolkit from the `@ability` methods of an object
      `Toolkit(target)` is a macro. For each method of `target`'s type annotated `@ability`
      (directly, or on a method it overrides) it makes an `Llm.Tool` named after the method,
      described by its `@about` annotation (or empty), whose schema is a JSON object with one
      required property per parameter of the first parameter list, each parameter type's
      schema summoned as `Schematic over JsonSchema`; and a dispatcher which decodes each
      argument (`Decodable in Json`), applies the method, and encodes the result (`Encodable
      in Json`). A method may have one explicit parameter list and, optionally, one contextual
      list, whose values are summoned where `Toolkit(…)` is written, so the model never sees
      them.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      object Greeter:
        @ability
        def shout(message: Text)(using suffix: Text): Text = t"$message$suffix"

      given suffix: Text = t"!"
      Toolkit(Greeter).invoke(t"shout", j"""{"message": "hello"}""")   // j""""hello!""""
      ```

      Each is a compile error: a target with no `@ability` method, a parameter with no JSON
      schema or decoder, a return type with no encoder, a contextual parameter nothing in
      scope provides, or a method with more than two parameter lists.
  entry #specs   the tools offered to the model: each a name, a description and an argument schema
  entry #invoke   runs the named tool with the model's JSON arguments, and encodes its result as JSON
      Raises `Llm.Error` with reason `Invalid` for a tool the toolkit does not have or an
      argument which is missing or does not decode; the session's tool loop turns such an error
      into an error-marked `ToolResult` for the model to read.

entry .internal   the macro implementation behind `Toolkit(…)`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the `Toolkit.apply` macro has a stable
      home.
  entry .argument   decodes one tool argument from JSON, or `Unset` when it is malformed
  entry .toolkit   the macro behind `Toolkit.apply`: `@ability` methods to specifications and a dispatcher

entry .Llm   the provider-neutral vocabulary: messages, replies, sessions, streams, tools, usage and errors
  detail
      `Llm` is the object which holds everything the dialects share. A conversation is a
      `.Session`, obtained only through a provider's `session` and reached as `llm`; a turn is
      a `.Message` of a `.Role` and a list of `.Content` blocks; a completed assistant turn is
      a `.Reply` with its `.Stop` reason and `.Usage`; a streamed turn is a `.Response` of
      `.Event`s; a tool is a `.Tool`; the knobs every wire expresses are `.Settings`; a
      failure is an `.Error`. A provider adapter is a `.Dialect`, which encodes an `.Exchange`
      to its wire and translates the reply or the stream back.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val question = Llm.Message(Llm.Role.User, t"How many bones are in the human hand?")

      val illustrated = Llm.Message
        ( Llm.Role.User,
          List
            ( Llm.Content.Textual(t"What is in this picture?"),
              Llm.Content.Graphic(Llm.Content.Source.Remote(url"https://example.com/hand.png")) ) )
      ```

      Identifiers are typed by what they name: an `Llm.Id[Llm.Reply]` is a reply's, an
      `Llm.Id[Llm.Request]` a batched request's, an `Llm.Id[Anthropic.Batch[?]]` a batch's,
      and none is a `Text`, so a reply's identifier cannot be handed to a method expecting a
      batch's. A batch of questions is a list of `.Request`s and answers as `.Outcome`s.
  refer sibylline.Llm.Session
  refer sibylline.Llm.Reply
  refer sibylline.Llm.Message
  refer sibylline.Llm.Content
  refer sibylline.Llm.Error
  refer sibylline.llm
  entry .Role   the two turn-taking roles: `User` and `Assistant`
      A conversation alternates between the two. The system prompt is not a role — two of the
      wire APIs carry it outside the message list — so it is set on the provider value with
      `prompted` and travels as a field of the `Exchange`, never as a message.
  entry .Id   an identifier typed by what it names: a reply, a batched request or a batch
    detail
        `Llm.Id[subject]` is an opaque type over `Text`: a provider mints a reply's
        (`Reply#id`, `Event.Started#id`) and a batch's (`Anthropic.Batch#id`) from the wire, and
        a caller mints a request's with `.request`, which checks it before any provider can
        reject it. The text is read back with `.text`, and distinct subjects never mix.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

        val id: Llm.Id[Llm.Request] = Llm.Id.request(t"aapl")
        id.text == t"aapl"
        val batch: Llm.Id[Anthropic.Batch[?]] = t"msgbatch_1".as[Llm.Id[Anthropic.Batch[?]]]
        ```

        An `Id` is `Showable` (as its text), encodable and decodable in `Text` and therefore
        in JSON, and inspects as `Llm.Id(…)` so that it is not mistaken for a bare `Text`.
    entry .request   a request identifier of the caller's choosing, checked before submission
        Accepts one to sixty-four characters from letters, digits, `_` and `-` — Anthropic's
        `custom_id` rule, the strictest of the wire APIs', applied for every dialect — and
        raises `Llm.Error` with reason `Invalid`, _the request identifier `text` is not
        permitted_, for anything else, so a bad identifier fails before a batch is sent.
    entry .text   the identifier's text
        An extension on any `Id[subject]`: `reply.id.let(_.text) == t"msg_1"`.
    entry .showable   shows an identifier as its text
    entry .encodable   encodes an identifier as text, and so as a JSON string
    entry .decodable   decodes any text as an identifier, unchecked
        Reading a stored batch identifier back: `text.as[Llm.Id[Anthropic.Batch[?]]]`. No rule
        is applied; `.request` is the checked way to mint a request's.
    entry .inspectable   inspects an identifier as `Llm.Id("…")`, distinguishing it from a `Text`
  entry .Request   one question in a batch: its identifier and its prompt
    detail
        `Llm.Request(id, prompt)` is what `Anthropic#elicitAll` takes a list of: each becomes
        its own exchange, with the provider value's system prompt, priming and settings, and
        its `Outcome` is keyed by `#id`, since the provider returns outcomes in any order.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

        val requests = List
          ( Llm.Request(Llm.Id.request(t"aapl"), t"Summarize your recommendation for AAPL."),
            Llm.Request(Llm.Id.request(t"msft"), t"Summarize your recommendation for MSFT.") )
        ```
    entry #id   the identifier its outcome will carry, made with `Llm.Id.request`
    entry #prompt   the user turn of the request's exchange
  entry .Outcome   what became of one batched request: its decoded answer, or the error standing in for it
    detail
        `Anthropic.Batch#outcomes()` returns one `Outcome[value]` per request, in the
        provider's order. `#result` is a `value | Llm.Error`: a succeeded request's reply
        decoded to the batch's type, or an `Llm.Error` for a request which errored (with the
        provider's reason), was canceled (`Provider("canceled")`), expired
        (`Provider("expired")`), or whose answer did not match its schema (`Malformed`). Match
        on the union:

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        batch.outcomes().each: outcome =>
          outcome.result match
            case appraisal: Appraisal => Out.println(t"${outcome.id}: ${appraisal.rating}")
            case error: Llm.Error     => Out.println(t"${outcome.id} failed: ${error.message}")
        ```
    entry #id   the request's own identifier, by which the outcome is matched to its question
    entry #result   the decoded answer, or the `Llm.Error` which stands in for it
  entry .Content   one block of a message, in the vocabulary every dialect translates to and from
    detail
        A message's content is a list of blocks. `Textual(text)` is prose; `Graphic(source)`
        an image and `Document(source)` a document (a PDF, say), each from a `.Source`;
        `ToolUse(id, tool, arguments)` a call the model made, with its arguments as `Json`;
        `ToolResult(id, content, failure)` the answer to that call, in a user turn, with
        `failure = true` marking an error the model should recover from; `Thinking(text,
        signature)` the model's reasoning where the provider exposes it, and `Redacted(data)`
        reasoning it withheld; and `Opaque(provider, json)` any provider block the vocabulary
        does not model, verbatim. A dialect replays its own provider's opaque blocks when the
        history is re-sent and drops another provider's, so a transcript is never corrupted and
        never lies.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val call = Llm.Content.ToolUse(t"toolu_1", t"price", j"""{"ticker": "AAPL"}""")
        val answer = Llm.Content.ToolResult(t"toolu_1", List(Llm.Content.Textual(t"211.5")))
        val failed = Llm.Content.ToolResult(t"toolu_1", List(Llm.Content.Textual(t"no such ticker")), failure = true)
        ```

        Not every block has a form on every wire: OpenAI's Chat Completions drops a
        `Document`, and Gemini carries no identifier on a tool call, so there the tool's name
        serves as the `ToolUse` id and a `ToolResult` addresses its call by that name.
    refer sibylline.Llm.Message
    entry .Source   where binary content lives: inline bytes with a media type, or a URL
        `Inline(data, mediaType)` carries the bytes themselves, rendered as base64 on the wire
        (or as a data URL, on a wire which takes only URLs); `Remote(url)` is an `HttpUrl` the
        provider fetches itself.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        Llm.Content.Graphic(Llm.Content.Source.Remote(url"https://example.com/hand.png"))
        ```
  entry .Message   one turn of a conversation: a role and a list of content blocks
    detail
        `Message(role, content)` is a user or assistant turn; `Message(role, text)` makes the
        common one-block textual turn. `Session#ask` and `Session#stream` accept a message as
        readily as text, which is how an image, a document or a tool result is sent, and
        `Session#record` appends one to the history without a round trip.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val plain = Llm.Message(Llm.Role.User, t"Tallest mountain in Estonia?")

        val illustrated = Llm.Message
          ( Llm.Role.User,
            List
              ( Llm.Content.Textual(t"What is in this picture?"),
                Llm.Content.Graphic(Llm.Content.Source.Remote(url"https://example.com/hand.png")) ) )
        ```
    refer sibylline.Llm.Content
    entry .apply   `Message(role, text)`: a turn of one textual block
    entry #role   `User` or `Assistant`
    entry #content   the turn's blocks, in order
  entry .Stop   why the model stopped emitting
      `Ended` is a natural end of turn; `Exhausted` the output-token limit; `ToolCall` a
      request to run a tool, which the session's tool loop dispatches on; `Refused` a refusal;
      `Sequence(text)` one of the `stopping` sequences, which is carried; `Filtered(detail)` a
      content filter; and `Other(code)` any reason the vocabulary does not model, with the
      provider's code kept. Decoding is total, so no stop reason is ever lost. A reply which
      contains a tool call is `ToolCall` on every dialect, even where the wire (Gemini) says
      it stopped normally.
  entry .Usage   token accounting: input and output counts, and the optional cache and reasoning counts
    detail
        `Usage(input, output, cacheRead, cacheWrite, reasoning)`: only the input and output
        counts are universal, and the rest are `Optional`, reported by some providers only —
        `cacheRead` and `cacheWrite` by Anthropic (and `cacheRead` by OpenAI), `reasoning` by
        Gemini. A reply's usage is the cost of that turn; `Session#usage` is the fold over
        every committed turn, and `.addable` is how it folds: an absent count plus an absent
        count stays absent, so a total is never invented.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Llm.Usage(1, 2) + Llm.Usage(3, 4)                                       // Usage(4, 6)
        Llm.Usage(1, 2, cacheRead = 10) + Llm.Usage(3, 4, cacheRead = 5, reasoning = 7)
        ```
    entry .addable   `usage + usage`: sums the counts, keeping an optional count absent when both are
    entry #input   the input tokens
    entry #output   the output tokens
    entry #cacheRead   the input tokens read from the provider's prompt cache, where reported
        What `Anthropic#caching` is for: a cached system prompt shows up here on every request
        which shares it.
    entry #cacheWrite   the input tokens written to the prompt cache, where reported
    entry #reasoning   the tokens spent thinking, where reported
  entry .Reply   one completed assistant turn: its message, stop reason, usage, model and identifier
    detail
        Pure data, so it may leave the session block. `#text` joins its textual blocks and
        `#toolCalls` picks out its `ToolUse` blocks; `#stop` says why the model stopped and
        `#usage` what the turn cost; `#model` and `#id` are what the provider reported, when it
        did.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        val reply: Llm.Reply = claude.session(llm.ask(t"Tallest mountain in Estonia?"))
        reply.text
        reply.stop == Llm.Stop.Ended
        reply.usage.output
        reply.id.let(_.text)
        ```
    refer sibylline.Llm.Session
    entry #message   the assistant message, with every content block the model sent
    entry #stop   why the model stopped: `Ended`, `Exhausted`, `ToolCall`, …
    entry #usage   the tokens this turn cost
    entry #model   the model the provider reported, or `Unset`
    entry #id   the reply's identifier as the provider issued it, or `Unset`
    entry #text   the reply's textual blocks, joined
        Thinking, tool calls and opaque blocks are left out; a reply of no text is empty.
    entry #toolCalls   the reply's `ToolUse` blocks, in order
        What the tool loop runs when `#stop` is `ToolCall`; empty for a reply which called no
        tool.
  entry .ToolChoice   whether the model may, must, must not, or must specifically call a tool
      `Auto` leaves the choice to the model, `Forbidden` prevents any call, `Required` demands
      one, and `Named(tool)` demands that tool. Set through `Settings#toolChoice`; `solicit`
      sets `Named("answer")` for its one forced turn on a dialect without native structured
      output. Every wire supports all four.
  entry .Settings   the request knobs every wire expresses
    detail
        `maxTokens`, `temperature`, `topP`, `stopSequences`, `toolChoice` and `iterations`,
        each optional: a knob left `Unset` is omitted from the request, so the provider's own
        default applies, rather than sent as a default of the library's. A provider value's
        `limit`, `warmth`, `sampling`, `stopping` and `iterating` set them one at a time; a
        provider-specific knob (a thinking budget, caching, a beta header) is a method of the
        provider value, not a field here. Anthropic's wire requires `max_tokens`, so an unset
        `maxTokens` is sent as 4096 there.
    entry #maxTokens   the most output tokens a turn may produce
    entry #temperature   the sampling temperature
    entry #topP   the nucleus-sampling threshold
    entry #stopSequences   the texts at which the model stops; empty for none
    entry #toolChoice   whether and which tool the model must call
    entry #iterations   the most tool-loop round trips one `ask` may make; 16 when unset
  entry .Tool   a tool the model may call: its name, its description and the JSON schema of its arguments
    detail
        `Tool(name, description, parameters)` is the wire specification only; the `Toolkit`
        macro produces one per `@ability` method, with the dispatcher which runs it. A provider
        value's `tooled(tools)` advertises tools in every turn without a toolkit to run them,
        for a program which handles `ToolCall` replies itself.
    refer sibylline.Toolkit
    entry #name   the name the model calls it by
    entry #description   what it does, as the model reads it
    entry #parameters   the JSON schema of its arguments, an object with one property per parameter
  entry .Exchange   a pure description of one request: everything a dialect needs to encode a wire call
    detail
        `Exchange(system, history, tools, settings, format)`: the system prompt, the whole
        history including the new turn, the tools on offer, the settings, and, when set, the
        JSON schema the reply's text must conform to. The session builds one per round trip and
        the dialect encodes it; a dialect which is not `structured` never sees a `format`. A
        program only meets an `Exchange` when it implements or fakes a `Dialect`.
    entry #system   the system prompt, or `Unset`
    entry #history   every message so far, ending with the turn being sent
    entry #tools   the tools offered in this turn
    entry #settings   the knobs for this turn
    entry #format   the JSON schema a structured reply must conform to, or `Unset`
  entry .Event   the neutral streaming vocabulary: blocks open, grow by increments and close
    detail
        Every wire's event stream reduces to the same index-addressed shape. `Started(id,
        model)` begins the message; `Opened(index, content)` opens a content block, as it
        first appears; `Delta(index, increment)` grows it by an `.Increment`; `Closed(index)`
        closes it; `Update(stop, usage)` carries message-level state, possibly several times
        (Anthropic reports input tokens at the start and output tokens at the end, and the
        accumulator sums them); and `Finished` ends the message. `Response#events` yields
        these, and `Response#reply()` folds them into a `Reply`; a stream which ends without
        `Finished`, or with a block still open, raises `Llm.Error(Interrupted)`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val events: List[Llm.Event] =
          List
            ( Llm.Event.Started(Unset, Unset),
              Llm.Event.Opened(0, Llm.Content.ToolUse(t"id1", t"price", j"{}")),
              Llm.Event.Delta(0, Llm.Event.Increment.Arguments(t"""{"tick""")),
              Llm.Event.Delta(0, Llm.Event.Increment.Arguments(t"""er": "AAPL"}""")),
              Llm.Event.Closed(0),
              Llm.Event.Update(Llm.Stop.ToolCall, Unset),
              Llm.Event.Finished )
        ```

        Text deltas fill the block they address; a tool call's arguments arrive as partial
        JSON which is parsed when the block closes (`Malformed` if it is not JSON by then).
    refer sibylline.Llm.Response
    entry .Increment   a fragment of an open block: text, thinking, a signature, or partial JSON arguments
        `Textual(text)` extends a text block, `Thinking(text)` a thinking block,
        `Signature(text)` a thinking block's signature, and `Arguments(json)` a tool call's
        arguments with partial JSON text, complete only when the block closes.
  entry .Dialect   the seam a provider adapter implements: encode an exchange, make the call, translate the reply
    detail
        A dialect speaks two methods: `#exchange` for one full reply, `#stream` for the
        event stream, both from a pure `Exchange`; `#name` names the wire, and `#structured`
        says whether it honours `Exchange#format` natively. The library's four dialects
        (Anthropic's Messages API, OpenAI's Chat Completions and Responses, Gemini) are private
        and are constructed by each provider's `Sessional`, capturing the HTTP capabilities and
        the caller's tactic, so the session never touches HTTP. The trait is public so that a
        test can fake one; `Session`'s constructor, which takes a dialect, is not, so a custom
        dialect cannot be driven through a `Session` from outside the library.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        class Scripted(answers: List[Llm.Reply]) extends Llm.Dialect:
          private val replies = scala.collection.mutable.Queue(answers.stdlib*)
          def name: Text = t"scripted"
          def exchange(turn: Llm.Exchange): Llm.Reply = replies.dequeue()

          def stream(turn: Llm.Exchange): Iterator[Llm.Event]^{this, scala.caps.any} =
            Iterator.from(List.iterator(List[Llm.Event]()))
        ```

        The shared HTTP engine behind the real dialects applies one retry policy: a 429, 503 or
        529 is retried three times, after the `retry-after` delay when the provider sends one
        and with exponential backoff (1, 2, 4 seconds) when it does not; any other failure is
        mapped through the dialect's error envelope and raised as `Llm.Error`.
    entry #name   the wire's name: `anthropic`, `openai`, `openai-responses`, `gemini`
    entry #exchange   one non-streamed round trip: the exchange to a `Reply`
    entry #stream   one streamed round trip: the exchange to an iterator of `Event`s
        The result captures the dialect and the fresh streaming state it advances, so its type
        is `Iterator[Event]^{this, caps.any}`; an implementation must declare the same.
    entry #structured   whether the wire constrains a reply to a JSON schema natively; `false` unless overridden
        Anthropic's dialect is `true`. When `false`, `solicit` obtains a structured answer
        through a forced tool call instead, and the caller sees no difference; a fake dialect
        which overrides it to `true` must return the JSON document as the reply's text.
  entry .Error   raised when a model request fails: unreachable, unauthorized, rate-limited, malformed, …
    detail
        Error 990, subcoded by `.Reason`: _the model request failed because `reason`:
        `detail`_. Every failure a provider, the network or the translation can produce is an
        `Llm.Error`; `#status` is the HTTP status when there was one, and `#retry` the
        `retry-after` delay in seconds on a rate limit or overload, the two facts a caller
        needs to decide whether and when to try again. A rate limit or overload has already
        been retried three times by the time it is raised. The `Tactic[Llm.Error]` is taken
        when `session` opens, so `strategies.throwUnsafely`, `safely` or `recover` around the
        session call handles every turn inside it:

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        recover:
          case error: Llm.Error => error.reason match
            case Llm.Error.Reason.Unauthorized => Out.println(t"check the API key")
            case Llm.Error.Reason.TooLarge     => Out.println(t"shorten the conversation")
            case other                         => Out.println(error.message)
        . protect:
            val reply = claude.session(llm.ask(t"Hello"))
            Out.println(reply.text)
        ```

        A wire error envelope is mapped onto a reason by its type or its status, and an error
        code which matches no reason is kept under `Provider(code)`, so the wire's own error is
        never lost.
    entry .Reason   the fixed set of failure kinds, each the error's subcode
        `Unreachable` (1), the provider could not be reached; `Unauthorized` (2), the API key
        was missing, invalid or expired; `Invalid` (3), the provider rejected the request as
        invalid — also an unknown tool, a malformed argument or a bad request identifier;
        `NotFound` (4), the model or endpoint does not exist; `TooLarge` (5), the conversation
        exceeds the context window; `RateLimited` (6); `Overloaded` (7); `Malformed` (8), the
        reply, a stream frame or a structured answer could not be interpreted; `Interrupted`
        (9), the stream ended before the message was complete; `ToolLoopExceeded` (10), the
        tool loop reached its `iterations` bound; and `Provider(code)` (11), an error code the
        provider reported which maps to none of the others. The numbers are frozen; a reason
        added later appends.
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as the message the error carries
    entry #reason   which kind of failure it was
    entry #detail   the provider's message, or the library's account of a local failure
    entry #status   the HTTP status, when there was one
    entry #retry   the `retry-after` delay in seconds, on a rate limit or overload which sent one
  entry .Response   a streamed assistant turn in flight: its events, its text, and the reply once drained
    detail
        `Session#stream` returns one. `#text` is an iterator of the text fragments, pulled
        synchronously off the connection as they arrive, and `#events` the finer-grained
        `Event`s; `#reply()` drains whatever remains, folds everything into a `Reply`, and
        commits the user and assistant turns to the session's history together. Every event
        pulled through any of the three feeds the same accumulator, so a partially-consumed
        stream still completes on `reply()`, and `reply()` is idempotent.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        claude.session:
          val response = llm.stream(t"Write a limerick about Tallinn.")
          response.text.each(Out.print(_))
          response.reply()
        ```

        Nothing is committed until `reply()` is called: an abandoned or failed stream leaves
        the history as it was, so a failed turn is simply re-asked from the previous turn
        boundary. The response borrows the session, so it is confined to the session's block
        and — since the session is exclusive — no other turn can begin while it is undrained;
        storing it in an outer variable is a compile error.
    refer sibylline.Llm.Session
    refer sibylline.Llm.Event
    entry #events   every streaming event, once, in order
    entry #text   the text deltas only, as they arrive
        For `response.text.each(Out.print(_))`; the thinking, tool-argument and signature
        increments are absorbed but not yielded.
    entry #reply   drains the stream, assembles the `Reply`, and commits the turn to the session
        Raises `Llm.Error(Interrupted)` if the stream ended before `Finished` or with a block
        still open, and `Malformed` if a tool call's streamed arguments were not JSON. A second
        call returns the same `Reply` without touching the stream.
  entry .Session   a conversation: the one mutable holder of its history and usage, confined to its block
    detail
        A session is opened on a provider value with `session` — `claude.session: …` — and
        reached inside the block as `llm`; it has no public constructor. `#ask` sends a turn
        and returns the `Reply`, committing both turns to `#history`; `#stream` sends a turn
        and returns a `Response` to read as it arrives; `solicit[T]` asks for a typed answer;
        `#record` seeds the history without a round trip; `#usage` is the fold of every
        committed turn's cost.

        With a `Toolkit` given in scope, `ask` runs the tool loop, and the history grows by
        every turn the loop makes:

        ```scala fuel
        frame
          label The question
          code
              import soundness.*

              val asked = Llm.Message(Llm.Role.User, t"Price AAPL?")
              val history: List[Llm.Message] = List(asked)
        frame
          label The model stops to call a tool
          code
              import soundness.*

              val asked = Llm.Message(Llm.Role.User, t"Price AAPL?")

              val called = Llm.Message
                ( Llm.Role.Assistant,
                  List(Llm.Content.ToolUse(t"toolu_1", t"price", j"""{"ticker": "AAPL"}""")) )

              val history: List[Llm.Message] = List(asked, called)
        frame
          label The loop runs the tool and reports its result
          code
              import soundness.*

              val asked = Llm.Message(Llm.Role.User, t"Price AAPL?")

              val called = Llm.Message
                ( Llm.Role.Assistant,
                  List(Llm.Content.ToolUse(t"toolu_1", t"price", j"""{"ticker": "AAPL"}""")) )

              val reported = Llm.Message
                ( Llm.Role.User,
                  List(Llm.Content.ToolResult(t"toolu_1", List(Llm.Content.Textual(t"211.5")))) )

              val history: List[Llm.Message] = List(asked, called, reported)
        frame
          label The model answers in text, and the loop ends
          code
              import soundness.*

              val asked = Llm.Message(Llm.Role.User, t"Price AAPL?")

              val called = Llm.Message
                ( Llm.Role.Assistant,
                  List(Llm.Content.ToolUse(t"toolu_1", t"price", j"""{"ticker": "AAPL"}""")) )

              val reported = Llm.Message
                ( Llm.Role.User,
                  List(Llm.Content.ToolResult(t"toolu_1", List(Llm.Content.Textual(t"211.5")))) )

              val answered = Llm.Message(Llm.Role.Assistant, t"AAPL is trading at 211.50 dollars.")
              val history: List[Llm.Message] = List(asked, called, reported, answered)
        ##
        ```

        That four-message history is what `llm.history` holds after one `ask` which made one
        tool call, and what the next `ask` sends in full: history is replayed by the client on
        every wire, never threaded server-side.

        A session is an exclusive, stateful capability: `ask`, `stream` and `record` are
        updates, a streamed `Response` borrows it, and the `session` block's result type is
        quantified outside the block, so returning the session, or a closure over it, is a
        compile error, while a `Reply`, a `history` snapshot or a `Usage` leaves freely. Every
        method raises `Llm.Error` through the tactic taken when the session was opened.
    refer sibylline.llm
    refer sibylline.solicit
    refer sibylline.Llm.Response
    refer sibylline.Toolkit
    entry #history   every committed message so far, as a snapshot which may leave the block
    entry #usage   the fold of every committed turn's `Usage`
    entry #record   appends a message to the history without a round trip
        Replaying a transcript, or seeding context: a recorded message is sent with every later
        turn but costs nothing until then. A provider value's `primed(messages)` does the same
        for every session opened on it.
    entry #ask   sends a turn and returns the reply, running the tool loop if a `Toolkit` is given
        `ask(text)` sends a one-block user turn; `ask(message)` any `Message`, which is how an
        image, a document or a hand-made tool result is sent. The user turn and the reply are
        committed to the history together, after the call has succeeded. If the given `Toolkit`
        has tools and the reply's stop is `ToolCall`, each call is invoked, its result (or, for
        a failed or unknown call, an error-marked result) is sent as the next user turn, and
        the exchange repeats until the model stops for another reason, every turn committed;
        `Settings#iterations` (`iterating`, default 16) bounds the round trips, beyond which
        `Llm.Error(ToolLoopExceeded)` is raised. With no tools on offer a `ToolCall` reply is
        returned for the caller to handle.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        claude.session:
          given Toolkit = broker
          llm.ask(t"Compare AAPL and MSFT.")
        ```
    entry #stream   sends a turn and returns a `Response` to read as it arrives
        `stream(text)` or `stream(message)`. Nothing is committed until the response's
        `reply()` is called; the response borrows the session, so no other turn can begin
        while it is undrained, and it cannot leave the block.
    entry #structured   whether this session's dialect constrains replies to a schema natively
        `true` on Anthropic; `solicit` reads it to choose between the native format and a
        forced tool call.

entry .Anthropic   a Claude model on Anthropic's Messages API: a pure, reusable value which opens sessions and batches
  detail
      `Anthropic(model, key)` names a model and carries the API key; every tuning method
      returns a new value, so a configured model is an immutable value which may be a `val`,
      shared and reused across sessions. It is the target of `session`, through the
      `.sessional` given, and additionally offers what the Messages API alone has: structured
      batches (`#elicitAll`, `#batch`), token counting (`#countTokens`), prompt caching
      (`#caching`), a version header (`#versioned`) and beta features (`#trying`).

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A model and its key
        code
            import soundness.*

            val claude = Anthropic(t"claude-sonnet-4-5", t"sk-ant-…")
      frame
        label A system prompt and an output limit
        code
            import soundness.*

            val claude = Anthropic(t"claude-sonnet-4-5", t"sk-ant-…")
              . prompted(t"Be terse.")
              . limit(1024)
      frame
        label The prompt cached at the provider, and a bounded tool loop
        code
            import soundness.*

            val claude = Anthropic(t"claude-sonnet-4-5", t"sk-ant-…")
              . prompted(t"Be terse.")
              . limit(1024)
              . caching
              . iterating(4)
      ##
      ```

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      claude.session:
        llm.ask(t"What is the tallest mountain in Estonia?")
      ```

      Opening a session needs an `Online`, an `Http.Backend`, an `Http.Event is Loggable` and
      a `Tactic[Llm.Error]` in scope (`internetAccess.online`, `httpBackends.javaNetHttp`,
      `logging.silentLogging`, `strategies.throwUnsafely`). The wire headers (`x-api-key`,
      `anthropic-version`, `anthropic-beta`) are declared as givens in the companion, so using
      `Anthropic` needs no imports. The Messages API is stateless per request, so a session is
      only memory, and its dialect is the one which constrains a structured reply natively, so
      `solicit[T]` sends the schema as the reply's format rather than a forced tool. A knob left
      unset is omitted from the request, except `max_tokens`, which the wire requires and which
      is sent as 4096 when `limit` has not been called.

      The class is deliberately not a case class: `Anthropic(…)` is typed as plain `Anthropic`,
      which is what the `Sessional` instance matches.
  refer sibylline.Llm.Session
  refer sibylline.Anthropic.Batch
  refer sibylline.solicit
  entry .apply   `Anthropic(model, key)`: a model on `api.anthropic.com`, version `2023-06-01`, nothing else set
  entry .xApiKey   the `x-api-key` header directive, so `xApiKey = key` names it in an HTTP request
      In implicit scope through the companion; used by the dialect, and available to a program
      which calls the API with telekinesis directly.
  entry .anthropicVersion   the `anthropic-version` header directive
  entry .anthropicBeta   the `anthropic-beta` header directive
  entry .Sessional   the `Sessional` instance which opens an `Llm.Session` on an `Anthropic` value
    detail
        The class behind `.sessional`: `Self` is `Anthropic`, `Result` is a fresh
        `Llm.Session^`, and `session(target)(block)` constructs the Messages dialect from the
        HTTP capabilities and tactic in scope and lends a session over it to the block. The
        `Result` capability is fresh for each call, so returning the session, or anything
        which captures it, from the block is rejected by the capture checker. A named class
        rather than an anonymous given so that its `Result` type is stated exactly.
    entry #Self   `Anthropic`
    entry #Result   `Llm.Session^`, the fresh session handle the block receives
    entry #session   opens a session on the target and runs the block with it
  entry .sessional   the given which makes `anthropic.session: …` resolve
      In implicit scope through the companion. It needs an `Online`, an `Http.Backend`, an
      `Http.Event is Loggable`, a `Tactic[Llm.Error]` and `Diagnostics` where `session` is
      called, and captures the first three.
  entry .Batch   a batch of structured questions on the Message Batches API, as last seen
    detail
        `Anthropic#elicitAll[T](requests)` submits one and `Anthropic#batch[T](id)` finds one
        again. A `Batch[T]` is a pure record of its `#id`, `#status` and `#counts`, and of the
        type each answer decodes to; consulting the provider — `#refresh()`, `#await()`,
        `#outcomes()` — takes the HTTP capabilities and tactic at the call, so a batch may be
        kept, passed around, or resumed from its identifier in a later process. The Message
        Batches API runs the requests asynchronously, over minutes to hours, at half the price
        of the Messages API.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        val requests = List
          ( Llm.Request(Llm.Id.request(t"aapl"), t"Summarize your recommendation for AAPL."),
            Llm.Request(Llm.Id.request(t"msft"), t"Summarize your recommendation for MSFT.") )

        val batch: Anthropic.Batch[Appraisal] = claude.elicitAll[Appraisal](requests)
        val ended = batch.await()

        ended.outcomes().each: outcome =>
          outcome.result match
            case appraisal: Appraisal => Out.println(t"${outcome.id}: ${appraisal.rating}")
            case error: Llm.Error     => Out.println(t"${outcome.id} failed: ${error.message}")
        ```

        Outcomes arrive in the provider's order, not the order of submission, which is why
        each carries its request's identifier.
    refer sibylline.Llm.Request
    refer sibylline.Llm.Outcome
    entry .Status   where a batch is in its life: `Processing`, `Canceling`, `Ended`, or `Other(code)`
        Decoding is total: a status the vocabulary does not model keeps the provider's code.
        `await` polls until `Ended`.
    entry .Counts   how many of the batch's requests are in each state
      detail
          `Counts(processing, succeeded, errored, canceled, expired)`; `processing` reaches
          zero when the batch has `Ended`.
      entry #processing   requests still running
      entry #succeeded   requests which produced a reply
      entry #errored   requests the provider rejected
      entry #canceled   requests canceled before they ran
      entry #expired   requests the batch expired before running
    entry #id   the batch's identifier, an `Llm.Id[Anthropic.Batch[?]]`, by which it is found again
    entry #status   the status as last seen; `refresh()` for the current one
    entry #counts   the request counts as last seen
    entry #refresh   fetches the batch's current status and counts, as a new `Batch`
        One `GET` of the batch resource; raises `Llm.Error` (`NotFound` for an identifier the
        provider does not know). The receiver is unchanged.
    entry #await   polls until the batch has ended, sleeping `seconds` (30 by default) between polls
        A plain blocking wait on the caller's thread, each poll a `refresh()`; returns the
        ended batch. A batch already `Ended` returns at once.
    entry #outcomes   every request's outcome, in the provider's order
        Reads the results file (JSON Lines) and decodes each line to an `Llm.Outcome[T]`: a
        succeeded request's reply text parsed as JSON and decoded to `T`, or an `Llm.Error`
        for one which errored, was canceled, expired, or whose answer did not match the
        schema. The batch should have `Ended`; raises `Llm.Error` if the results are not yet
        available.
  entry #model   the model's identifier, as given to `Anthropic(model, key)`
  entry #system   the system prompt set by `#prompted`, or `Unset`
  entry #settings   the `Llm.Settings` the tuning methods have built up
  entry #priming   the history every session opened on this value starts from
  entry #tools   the tools advertised in every turn, set by `#tooled`
  entry #cached   whether the system prompt is marked as a prompt-cache breakpoint
  entry #prompted   a copy with a system prompt
      The system prompt travels outside the message list, as the Messages API takes it.
  entry #caching   a copy whose system prompt is a prompt-cache breakpoint
      Sends the system prompt as a content block with ephemeral cache control, so every request
      sharing it — each turn of a long session, or each request of a batch — reads the prefix
      from the provider's cache; `Llm.Usage#cacheRead` on each reply reports how much was read.
      Only meaningful after `#prompted`.
  entry #limit   a copy with a maximum number of output tokens
      Sent as `max_tokens`; 4096 is sent when this has not been called, since the wire
      requires a value.
  entry #warmth   a copy with a sampling temperature
  entry #sampling   a copy with a nucleus-sampling threshold (`top_p`)
  entry #stopping   a copy with stop sequences
      A reply which ended at one has `Llm.Stop.Sequence(text)` as its stop reason.
  entry #iterating   a copy with a bound on the tool loop's round trips per `ask`; 16 unless set
  entry #primed   a copy whose sessions begin with the given history
      Replaying a transcript: the messages are sent with every turn and cost nothing until
      then. `Llm.Session#record` adds to one session instead.
  entry #tooled   a copy which advertises the given `Llm.Tool`s in every turn
      Specifications only: with no `Toolkit` given to run them, a `ToolCall` reply is returned
      to the caller. A `Toolkit` given in the session block offers its own tools in addition.
  entry #on   a copy addressing a different base URL
      For a proxy or a gateway speaking the Messages API; the default is
      `https://api.anthropic.com`.
  entry #versioned   a copy sending a different `anthropic-version` header; `2023-06-01` by default
  entry #trying   a copy sending an `anthropic-beta` header, to opt into a beta feature
  entry #elicitAll   submits many structured questions as one batch, answered later at half the price
      `claude.elicitAll[T](requests)` makes one exchange per `Llm.Request` — this value's
      system prompt, priming and settings, the request's prompt as the user turn, and the
      schema derived from `T` as the reply's format — and posts them to the Message Batches API,
      returning an `Anthropic.Batch[T]` in its initial `Processing` state. Needs `T is
      Schematic over JsonSchema` and `T is Json.Decodable`, plus the HTTP capabilities and a
      `Tactic[Llm.Error]`. Each request's identifier should come from `Llm.Id.request`, which
      applies the `custom_id` rule before anything is sent.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      val batch = claude.prompted(t"Be terse.").elicitAll[Appraisal](requests)
      batch.id.text   // keep this to resume the batch later
      ```
  entry #batch   a batch submitted earlier, in this process or another, found again by its identifier
      `claude.batch[T](id)` fetches the batch's current status and counts; `T` is the type
      its answers decode to, which the caller must name again, since the provider does not
      record it. Raises `Llm.Error(NotFound)` for an unknown identifier.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      val resumed = claude.batch[Appraisal](t"msgbatch_1".as[Llm.Id[Anthropic.Batch[?]]])
      resumed.status
      ```
  entry #countTokens   the input tokens a prospective conversation would cost, without running it
      `claude.countTokens(messages, system)` calls the `count_tokens` endpoint with this
      model, the messages and an optional system prompt, and returns the count, so a program
      can decide whether a document fits before sending it. Raises `Llm.Error(Malformed)` if
      the count is missing from the reply.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      claude.countTokens(List(question), system = t"Be terse.")
      ```

entry .OpenAI   a model on OpenAI's Chat Completions API, or any compatible server: a pure, reusable value
  detail
      `OpenAI(model, key)` names a model at `api.openai.com`; `OpenAI.compatible(base, model,
      key)` names one on any server speaking the same wire — vLLM, Ollama, Groq, Mistral and
      the rest — where only the base URL changes and a local server may need no key at all.
      Tuning methods return new values, and the value is the target of `session` through the
      `.sessional` given. `#responses` is the same model spoken to through OpenAI's Responses
      API instead.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val gpt = OpenAI(t"gpt-5", t"sk-…").warmth(0.2)
      val local = OpenAI.compatible(url"http://localhost:11434/v1", t"llama3")
      ```

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      local.session:
        llm.ask(t"What is the tallest mountain in Estonia?")
      ```

      The key, when there is one, is sent as a bearer token; the system prompt becomes a
      leading `system` message; a streamed request asks for the final usage chunk; a tool call's
      arguments, which this wire carries as a string of JSON, are parsed into `Json`. The Chat
      dialect is not `structured`, so `solicit[T]` forces a call to a synthetic `answer` tool.
      A `Document` block has no form on this wire and is dropped. The class is not a case
      class, so `OpenAI(…)` is typed as plain `OpenAI`, which the `Sessional` instance matches.
  refer sibylline.Llm.Session
  refer sibylline.OpenAI.Responses
  entry .apply   `OpenAI(model, key)`: a model on `api.openai.com/v1`, nothing else set
  entry .compatible   `OpenAI.compatible(base, model, key)`: the same wire on another server, the key optional
      `OpenAI.compatible(url"http://localhost:11434/v1", t"llama3")` for a keyless local
      server; with a key, it is sent as a bearer token. Endpoints are addressed beneath the
      base, so it includes the `/v1` where the server expects one.
  entry .Sessional   the `Sessional` instance which opens an `Llm.Session` on an `OpenAI` value
    detail
        The class behind `.sessional`: `Self` is `OpenAI`, `Result` a fresh `Llm.Session^`,
        and `session(target)(block)` constructs the Chat Completions dialect from the HTTP
        capabilities and tactic in scope and lends a session over it to the block, which cannot
        return it.
    entry #Self   `OpenAI`
    entry #Result   `Llm.Session^`, the fresh session handle the block receives
    entry #session   opens a session on the target and runs the block with it
  entry .sessional   the given which makes `openai.session: …` resolve
      In implicit scope through the companion; needs an `Online`, an `Http.Backend`, an
      `Http.Event is Loggable`, a `Tactic[Llm.Error]` and `Diagnostics` where `session` is
      called.
  entry .Responses   an `OpenAI` value spoken to through the Responses API instead of Chat Completions
    detail
        Obtained with `OpenAI(model, key).responses`: the same credentials, model and knobs,
        a different wire shape — item-based input, with the system prompt as `instructions`,
        and semantic streaming events. It is the target of `session` through its own
        `.sessional`. History is replayed by the client on every turn, as in every other
        dialect; a reasoning item in the output is dropped, a refusal becomes text, and any
        other item is kept as `Llm.Content.Opaque("openai-responses", …)`. Like the Chat
        dialect it is not `structured`, so `solicit[T]` forces the `answer` tool.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        gpt.responses.session:
          llm.ask(t"What is the tallest mountain in Estonia?")
        ```

        The value has no tuning methods of its own: configure the `OpenAI` value first, then
        take `responses`.
    entry .Sessional   the `Sessional` instance which opens an `Llm.Session` on a `Responses` value
      detail
          `Self` is `OpenAI.Responses`, `Result` a fresh `Llm.Session^`; `session` constructs
          the Responses dialect over the underlying `OpenAI` value's settings.
      entry #Self   `OpenAI.Responses`
      entry #Result   `Llm.Session^`, the fresh session handle the block receives
      entry #session   opens a session on the target and runs the block with it
    entry .sessional   the given which makes `openai.responses.session: …` resolve
        In implicit scope through the companion; needs the same `Online`, `Http.Backend`,
        `Http.Event is Loggable`, `Tactic[Llm.Error]` and `Diagnostics` as `OpenAI.sessional`.
  entry #model   the model's identifier
  entry #system   the system prompt set by `#prompted`, or `Unset`
  entry #settings   the `Llm.Settings` the tuning methods have built up
  entry #priming   the history every session opened on this value starts from
  entry #tools   the tools advertised in every turn, set by `#tooled`
  entry #prompted   a copy with a system prompt, sent as a leading `system` message
  entry #limit   a copy with a maximum number of output tokens (`max_completion_tokens`)
  entry #warmth   a copy with a sampling temperature
  entry #sampling   a copy with a nucleus-sampling threshold (`top_p`)
  entry #stopping   a copy with stop sequences
  entry #iterating   a copy with a bound on the tool loop's round trips per `ask`; 16 unless set
  entry #primed   a copy whose sessions begin with the given history
  entry #tooled   a copy which advertises the given `Llm.Tool`s in every turn, without running them
  entry #responses   the same model, credentials and knobs through the Responses API
      Returns an `OpenAI.Responses`, whose `session` speaks `/responses` rather than
      `/chat/completions`.

entry .Gemini   a model on Google's Gemini API: a pure, reusable value which opens sessions
  detail
      `Gemini(model, key)` names a model on `generativelanguage.googleapis.com` and carries
      the API key, sent as the `x-goog-api-key` header. Tuning methods return new values, and
      the value is the target of `session` through the `.sessional` given.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val gemini = Gemini(t"gemini-2.5-pro", t"AIza…").prompted(t"Be terse.").limit(256)
      ```

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      gemini.session:
        llm.ask(t"What is the tallest mountain in Estonia?")
      ```

      The wire's conventions are translated: the system prompt becomes the system instruction,
      the assistant role is `model`, a reply containing a function call is `Llm.Stop.ToolCall`
      even though the API says it stopped normally, a safety or recitation block is
      `Llm.Stop.Filtered(code)`, and the reasoning token count is reported as
      `Llm.Usage#reasoning`. Function calls carry no identifier on this wire, so a `ToolUse`
      block's id is the tool's name, and a `ToolResult` addresses its call by that name. The
      dialect is not `structured`, so `solicit[T]` forces a call to a synthetic `answer` tool.
      The class is not a case class, so `Gemini(…)` is typed as plain `Gemini`, which the
      `Sessional` instance matches.
  refer sibylline.Llm.Session
  entry .apply   `Gemini(model, key)`: a model on `generativelanguage.googleapis.com`, nothing else set
  entry .xGoogApiKey   the `x-goog-api-key` header directive, so `xGoogApiKey = key` names it in an HTTP request
      In implicit scope through the companion.
  entry .Sessional   the `Sessional` instance which opens an `Llm.Session` on a `Gemini` value
    detail
        The class behind `.sessional`: `Self` is `Gemini`, `Result` a fresh `Llm.Session^`,
        and `session(target)(block)` constructs the Gemini dialect from the HTTP capabilities
        and tactic in scope and lends a session over it to the block, which cannot return it.
    entry #Self   `Gemini`
    entry #Result   `Llm.Session^`, the fresh session handle the block receives
    entry #session   opens a session on the target and runs the block with it
  entry .sessional   the given which makes `gemini.session: …` resolve
      In implicit scope through the companion; needs an `Online`, an `Http.Backend`, an
      `Http.Event is Loggable`, a `Tactic[Llm.Error]` and `Diagnostics` where `session` is
      called.
  entry #model   the model's identifier, addressed in the endpoint path
  entry #system   the system prompt set by `#prompted`, or `Unset`
  entry #settings   the `Llm.Settings` the tuning methods have built up
  entry #priming   the history every session opened on this value starts from
  entry #tools   the tools advertised in every turn, set by `#tooled`
  entry #prompted   a copy with a system prompt, sent as the system instruction
  entry #limit   a copy with a maximum number of output tokens (`maxOutputTokens`)
  entry #warmth   a copy with a sampling temperature
  entry #sampling   a copy with a nucleus-sampling threshold (`topP`)
  entry #stopping   a copy with stop sequences
  entry #iterating   a copy with a bound on the tool loop's round trips per `ask`; 16 unless set
  entry #primed   a copy whose sessions begin with the given history
  entry #tooled   a copy which advertises the given `Llm.Tool`s in every turn, without running them
  entry #on   a copy addressing a different base URL
      For a proxy or a gateway speaking the Gemini API; the `v1beta/models/…` path is
      appended beneath it.
